// Investment Readiness Assessment: chapters 8-10, annexes and references
const back = [
  { h: 1, t: "Readiness for Financial Products" },
  { h: 2, t: "The Effect of Seasonality on Debt" },
  { p: "Seasonality is the single most important factor in matching products to R-NBT MSMEs. The example below shows why. It is illustrative and uses assumed figures, not data from the diagnostic." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Illustration: debt service for a guiding and transfer enterprise under three repayment structures",
    cols: [2426, 2200, 2200, 2200],
    head: ["Assumptions and results", "Standard amortising loan", "Seasonal amortisation", "Revenue-based finance"],
    rows: [
      ["Finance amount", "USD 20,000", "USD 20,000", "USD 20,000"],
      ["Terms", "24 monthly instalments at 20% a year", "Same total cost, paid in the 5 peak months of 2 seasons", "15% of monthly revenue until 1.3 times the amount is repaid"],
      ["Payment in a peak month (revenue USD 9,000)", "USD 1,018 (11% of revenue)", "USD 2,443 (27% of revenue)", "USD 1,350 (15% of revenue)"],
      ["Payment in a low-season month (revenue USD 2,140)", "USD 1,018 (48% of revenue)", "Nil", "USD 321 (15% of revenue)"],
      ["Total repaid", "USD 24,430", "USD 24,430", "USD 26,000"],
      ["Time to repay", "24 months", "Two peak seasons", "About 35 months at constant revenue"],
      ["Who bears a bad season", "Borrower", "Borrower (but cash is collected when available)", "Shared with financier"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "Consider a guiding and transfer enterprise that borrows USD 20,000. Under a **standard amortising loan** of 24 monthly instalments at 20% a year, it pays USD 1,018 every month. In a peak month with revenue of USD 9,000, that is 11% of revenue; in a low-season month with revenue of about USD 2,140, it is 48%. The enterprise repays USD 24,430 over 24 months and bears the full risk of a bad season." },
      { p: "Under **seasonal amortisation** with the same total cost, payments are made only in the five peak months of two seasons. Each peak-month payment is USD 2,443, or 27% of revenue, and nothing is due in the low season. The total repaid is again USD 24,430, over two peak seasons. The borrower still bears the risk of a bad season, but the lender collects cash when the enterprise has it." },
      { p: "Under **revenue-based finance**, the enterprise pays 15% of monthly revenue until it has repaid 1.3 times the amount borrowed: USD 1,350 in a peak month and USD 321 in a low-season month. It repays USD 26,000 in total, over about 35 months if revenue stays constant, and the financier shares the risk of a bad season." },
    ] },
  { p: "Assumptions: annual revenue of USD 60,000, 75% earned in five peak months (USD 9,000 a month) and 25% over seven low-season months (about USD 2,140 a month). Under a standard monthly schedule, nearly half of low-season revenue goes to debt service, before fixed costs, which is the point at which seasonal MSMEs default or stop trading. Seasonal amortisation removes the low-season burden at no extra cost but concentrates payments in the peak. Revenue-based finance shares the risk of a weak season with the financier and costs slightly more in total, but requires reliable revenue data, which in turn requires digital payments or anchor-buyer receipts." },
  { p: "**Conclusion.** For most R-NBT MSMEs, debt is viable only if repayment follows cash flow. Seasonal schedules, low-season grace periods and receivables finance are the most practical adjustments for banks and MFIs. Revenue-based finance is appropriate where revenue is observable (card, mobile-money or booking-platform receipts, or payments from anchor buyers) and is a natural fit for guiding, transport and accommodation enterprises in Tiers 2 and 3. Because contracts are slow to enforce in court in most KAZA states (Section 7.1), receivables and revenue-based structures work best when the anchor buyer or payment platform pays the financier directly." },
  { h: 2, t: "MSME Readiness by Product" },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "MSME readiness for financial products",
    cols: [2000, 1000, 2200, 3826],
    head: ["Product", "Ready tiers", "Fit with seasonality", "Assessment"],
    rows: [
      ["Grants and matching grants", "0-1", "Neutral", "Appropriate for certification, equipment and pilots; should be tied to milestones and convert to repayable finance where possible"],
      ["Savings-group and micro-loans", "0-1", "Good if loan cycles follow seasons", "Already used, especially by women; small and short; linkage to MFIs can extend size"],
      ["Contract-backed working capital and receivables finance", "2", "Good (repayment from buyer payment)", "Best near-term fit for suppliers to anchors (food, lodge-linked goods, transport, cleaning)"],
      ["Asset finance and leasing", "1-2", "Medium; needs seasonal schedule", "Fits guides, transport and processing equipment; asset is the security"],
      ["Standard term loans", "3", "Poor with monthly amortisation", "Few MSMEs ready; viable with seasonal schedule and guarantee"],
      ["Guaranteed loans", "2-3", "Depends on underlying loan", "Guarantees de-risk lenders rather than help MSMEs directly; tourism windows in national schemes would widen access"],
      ["Revenue-based finance", "2-3", "Very good", "Suits enterprises with observable revenue; not yet offered by KAZA lenders"],
      ["Quasi-equity and equity", "3", "Good (no fixed repayment)", "Relevant to a small number of growth enterprises and cooperatives; high transaction cost relative to ticket size; ownership rules in Botswana and Zimbabwe limit foreign equity in some chains"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**For Tiers 0 and 1,** grants and matching grants are neutral with respect to seasonality and are appropriate for certification, equipment and pilots; they should be tied to milestones and, where possible, convert to repayable finance. Savings-group and micro-loans suit the same tiers and fit seasonality well if loan cycles follow the seasons; they are already used, especially by women, but are small and short, and linkage to MFIs can increase their size. **Asset finance and leasing** suit Tiers 1 and 2 and fit guides, transport and processing equipment, with the asset as security, but their fit with seasonality is only medium unless repayment schedules are seasonal." },
      { p: "**For Tier 2,** contract-backed working capital and receivables finance are the best near-term fit for suppliers to anchors in food, lodge-linked goods, transport and cleaning, and fit seasonality well because repayment comes from the buyer's payment. Guaranteed loans suit Tiers 2 and 3; their fit with seasonality depends on the underlying loan, and they de-risk lenders rather than help MSMEs directly, so tourism windows in national schemes would widen access. Revenue-based finance, also for Tiers 2 and 3, fits seasonality very well and suits enterprises with observable revenue, but no KAZA lender yet offers it." },
      { p: "**For Tier 3,** standard term loans fit seasonality poorly when they are repaid in equal monthly instalments; few MSMEs are ready for them, and they become viable with a seasonal schedule and a guarantee. Quasi-equity and equity fit seasonality well because there is no fixed repayment, but they are relevant only to a small number of growth enterprises and cooperatives, transaction costs are high relative to ticket size, and ownership rules in Botswana and Zimbabwe limit foreign equity in some chains." },
    ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Financial Institution Readiness by Product" },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Financial institution readiness to offer suitable products",
    cols: [2000, 2500, 1500, 3026],
    head: ["Product", "Likely providers", "Readiness", "What is needed"],
    rows: [
      ["Contract-backed supplier finance", "Stanbic (BW, ZM, ZW); other commercial banks", "Medium", "Replication of the Wilderness-Stanbic model; standard tripartite contract templates with payment ceded to the lender; anchor commitment"],
      ["Seasonal working capital and term loans", "Banks, CEDA, CEEC, DBN, MFIs", "Low-Medium", "Product redesign, staff training, risk-sharing for early portfolios"],
      ["Asset finance and leasing", "Bank asset-finance units, leasing companies, green credit lines (EIF, IDBZ)", "Medium", "Smaller ticket sizes; vendor partnerships; seasonal schedules"],
      ["Partial credit guarantees", "CGS Namibia, ZCGS, ECGC, FGC; AGF regionally", "Medium (none in Botswana)", "Tourism or KAZA windows; acceptance of contracts and leaseholds as security; seasonal grace periods"],
      ["Micro-loans and group lending", "MFIs, women's and youth banks, savings-group linkage", "Medium", "Linkage programmes; seasonal loan cycles; digital channels"],
      ["Revenue-based finance", "Impact funds, fintechs, DFIs through intermediaries", "Low", "Pilot vehicle; revenue data from payment platforms or anchors; first-loss capital"],
      ["Equity and quasi-equity", "BDC, CEDA Venture Capital, impact investors; ZEEX in Zimbabwe", "Low", "Pipeline of Tier 3 enterprises; small-ticket fund or facility"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "Financial institutions are moderately ready for four products. **Contract-backed supplier finance** could be offered by Stanbic in Botswana, Zambia and Zimbabwe and by other commercial banks; it needs replication of the Wilderness-Stanbic model, standard tripartite contract templates in which payment is ceded to the lender, and anchor commitment. **Asset finance and leasing**, through bank asset-finance units, leasing companies and green credit lines such as those of EIF and IDBZ, need smaller ticket sizes, vendor partnerships and seasonal schedules. **Micro-loans and group lending** by MFIs, women's and youth banks and through savings-group linkage need linkage programmes, seasonal loan cycles and digital channels. **Partial credit guarantees** from CGS Namibia, ZCGS, ECGC and FGC, and regionally from AGF, are also moderately ready, although Botswana has no national scheme; they need tourism or KAZA windows, acceptance of contracts and leaseholds as security, and seasonal grace periods." },
      { p: "Readiness is lower for the remaining products. **Seasonal working capital and term loans** from banks, CEDA, CEEC, DBN and MFIs are at low to medium readiness and need product redesign, staff training and risk-sharing for early portfolios. **Revenue-based finance**, which impact funds, fintechs and DFIs could offer through intermediaries, is at low readiness and needs a pilot vehicle, revenue data from payment platforms or anchors, and first-loss capital. **Equity and quasi-equity** from BDC, CEDA Venture Capital, impact investors and, in Zimbabwe, the new ZEEX exchange are also at low readiness and need a pipeline of Tier 3 enterprises and a small-ticket fund or facility." },
    ] },

  { h: 1, t: "Scalable Women- and Youth-Led Models" },
  { p: "The evidence contains few enterprise cases, and most rest on single interviews. The models below show investment potential and are relevant to women or youth. Each is classified by model type, meaning its organisational form, value chain and value chain node (Section 2.2). Where the evidence does not state who leads the enterprise, this is noted." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Women- and youth-relevant models with investment potential",
    cols: [1600, 1500, 2400, 1500, 2026],
    head: ["Model", "Model type", "Evidence", "Investment potential", "Finance fit and what to verify"],
    rows: [
      ["Women's forest-products cooperative (Zambia)", "Women-led producer and processing cooperative (nature-positive products; node B)", "15 members; paid to harvest rather than fell forest; sells mungongo oil and baobab coffee to Livingstone hotels and export buyers; unsolicited bulk-oil export interest from Europe and South Africa (MD p. 73, 101)", "High: proven demand exceeds supply; women-led by definition", "Certification grant (about ZMW 30,000), processing equipment, export working capital; verify volumes, margins and governance"],
      ["Craft hub aggregating rural artisans (Maun, Botswana)", "Artisan aggregation hub (crafts; node B)", "Connects more than 100 rural artisans; export certifications; upcycles bottle tops and fabric into homeware; no committed international buyers (MD p. 69)", "Medium: aggregation in place; buyer gap", "Buyer brokerage and product design before finance; verify gender mix (likely women-majority, not stated)"],
      ["Elephant Aware Economy (Eastern Panhandle, Botswana)", "Community conservation enterprise cluster (nature-positive products; nodes A-B)", "Low-tillage farming cooperative selling at a premium; honey enterprise using bees as elephant deterrent; dung paper; honey tasting flagged as high-opportunity niche (MD p. 69-70, 115)", "Medium: premium positioning and conservation story", "Grants and blended finance; experience products; verify leadership and member profile"],
      ["Community trust waste and upcycling enterprise (Namibia)", "Community-trust social enterprise (waste and circular economy; nodes B, E)", "Grew from a waste gap into a sewing workshop, recycled-can aluminium workshop and beehive initiative (MD p. 71, 115)", "Medium: diversified services and products", "Equipment and working capital; service contracts with lodges and towns; verify women and youth roles"],
      ["Sijwa beehive project (Namibia)", "Conservancy-linked benefit-sharing enterprise (honey; nodes A, E)", "Returns 12% of sales to the conservancy (MD p. 115)", "Medium: replicable benefit-sharing model", "Revolving fund or group finance; verify volumes and buyers"],
      ["Produce aggregation for lodges (Hwange, Kasane, Livingstone)", "Producer cooperative supplying anchors under contract (food; nodes A-C)", "Named clearest near-term opportunity; aggregation would give volume and bargaining power (MD p. 68, 75). In Livingstone, a hotel buys directly from smallholders, and a women's mushroom farm sells most of its produce to hotels under long-term contracts (operator-reported)", "High: demand confirmed by anchors; working precedent in Livingstone", "Contract-backed working capital and irrigation finance; design as women- or youth-led cooperatives; verify contract terms and prices"],
      ["Citizen supplier development with an anchor (Botswana)", "Anchor-led supplier development programme (several chains; node C)", "Wilderness programme: 68 businesses enrolled; more than BWP 6.2 million of new contracts for citizen-owned businesses after the 2024 expo, passing BWP 8 million by mid-2025; linked to Stanbic contract finance and LEA support", "High: replicable with other operators", "Contract-backed finance; verify share of women- and youth-led suppliers"],
      ["Youth guiding, activity and transfer enterprises (Victoria Falls-Chobe corridor)", "Owner-operator service micro-enterprise (guiding and transport; node D)", "Largest survey category; guiding, transport and cultural experiences commonly combined (MD p. 61, 118)", "Medium-High: low start-up cost, clear demand", "Skills certification, asset leasing, revenue-based finance; verify age profile"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { h: 3, t: "Models with high investment potential" },
      { p: "**Women's forest-products cooperative (Zambia).** *Model type: women-led producer and processing cooperative in nature-positive products (node B).* The cooperative has 15 members, pays them to harvest rather than fell the forest, and sells mungongo oil and baobab coffee to Livingstone hotels and export buyers; it has received unsolicited bulk-oil export interest from Europe and South Africa (MD p. 73, 101). Its investment potential is high because demand already exceeds supply and it is women-led by definition. It needs a certification grant of about ZMW 30,000, processing equipment and export working capital; volumes, margins and governance should be verified." },
      { p: "**Produce aggregation for lodges (Hwange, Kasane and Livingstone).** *Model type: producer cooperative supplying anchors under contract in food (nodes A to C).* Anchor lodges name irrigated produce supply as the clearest near-term opportunity, and aggregation would give producers volume and bargaining power (MD p. 68, 75). In Livingstone, a hotel already buys directly from smallholders, and a women's mushroom farm sells most of its produce to hotels under long-term contracts (operator-reported). The potential is high because anchors have confirmed demand and a working precedent exists. Contract-backed working capital and irrigation finance fit this model, which should be designed as women- or youth-led cooperatives; contract terms and prices should be verified." },
      { p: "**Citizen supplier development with an anchor (Botswana).** *Model type: anchor-led supplier development programme spanning several chains (node C).* Wilderness's programme has enrolled 68 businesses, and its 2024 Local Supplier Expo led to more than BWP 6.2 million of new contracts for citizen-owned businesses, passing BWP 8 million by mid-2025; it is linked to Stanbic contract finance and LEA support. The potential is high because the model can be replicated with other operators. Contract-backed finance fits it; the share of women- and youth-led suppliers should be verified." },
      { h: 3, t: "Models with medium to high potential" },
      { p: "**Youth guiding, activity and transfer enterprises (Victoria Falls-Chobe corridor).** *Model type: owner-operator service micro-enterprise in guiding and transport (node D).* Guiding is the largest category in the MSME survey, and guiding, transport and cultural experiences are commonly combined (MD p. 61, 118). Low start-up costs and clear demand give medium to high potential. Skills certification, asset leasing and revenue-based finance fit these enterprises; the age profile of operators should be verified." },
      { h: 3, t: "Models with medium potential" },
      { p: "**Craft hub aggregating rural artisans (Maun, Botswana).** *Model type: artisan aggregation hub in crafts (node B).* The hub connects more than 100 rural artisans, holds export certifications and upcycles bottle tops and fabric into homeware, but has no committed international buyers (MD p. 69). Aggregation is in place but the buyer gap limits its potential, so buyer brokerage and product design should come before finance. The gender mix, probably women-majority, is not stated and should be verified." },
      { p: "**Elephant Aware Economy (Eastern Panhandle, Botswana).** *Model type: community conservation enterprise cluster in nature-positive products (nodes A and B).* It combines a low-tillage farming cooperative selling at a premium, a honey enterprise that uses bees to deter elephants and dung paper, and honey tasting has been flagged as a high-opportunity niche (MD p. 69-70, 115). Premium positioning and a conservation story give it medium potential. Grants, blended finance and experience products fit it; its leadership and member profile should be verified." },
      { p: "**Community trust waste and upcycling enterprise (Namibia).** *Model type: community-trust social enterprise in waste and the circular economy (nodes B and E).* It grew from a waste gap into a sewing workshop, a recycled-can aluminium workshop and a beehive initiative (MD p. 71, 115). Its diversified services and products give medium potential. It needs equipment, working capital and service contracts with lodges and towns; the roles of women and youth should be verified." },
      { p: "**Sijwa beehive project (Namibia).** *Model type: conservancy-linked benefit-sharing enterprise in honey (nodes A and E).* It returns 12% of sales to the conservancy (MD p. 115), a replicable benefit-sharing model with medium potential. A revolving fund or group finance would fit it; volumes and buyers should be verified." },
    ] },
  { p: "The Maun craft hub and the Zambian cooperative illustrate the two main barriers to scale: a buyer gap in the first case and a supply and certification gap in the second. Finance alone solves neither; both need finance packaged with market linkage and technical support. The Livingstone and Wilderness cases show the reverse: once a buyer commits, smallholders and citizen suppliers can be brought into contract and then into finance." },

  { h: 1, t: "Recommended Capacity-Building Interventions" },
  { h: 2, t: "A Tiered Readiness Pathway" },
  { p: "Support should be organised as a pathway through which MSMEs graduate from tier to tier, with the support package and finance changing at each step. A common readiness diagnostic, applied by BSOs, would place each MSME in a tier and track progress. At every tier, support should combine delivery modes rather than rely on classroom training: technical assistance, on-the-job training, mentoring and coaching, workshops, and benchmarking or exchange visits (Section 6.1)." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Tiered readiness pathway",
    cols: [1800, 2900, 2300, 2026],
    head: ["Tier", "Support package", "Finance pathway", "Graduation milestone"],
    rows: [
      ["Tier 0: Pre-enterprise", "Group formation and governance; savings-group linkage; financial literacy; product and market awareness", "Savings groups; small grants and in-kind inputs", "Regular sales; group or individual records for six months"],
      ["Tier 1: Emerging", "Bookkeeping and mobile-money use; costing and pricing; quality and product development; registration support", "Savings-linked micro-loans; matching grants; small asset leases", "Registered; first repeat orders from a buyer; basic records"],
      ["Tier 2: Supplier-ready", "Supplier development with anchor buyers; quality assurance, standards and productivity; compliance (tax, VAT, insurance, certification); contracting and negotiation; cash-flow planning", "Contract-backed working capital; receivables and asset finance; guaranteed loans", "Contract or repeat orders for a full season; tax clearance; simple accounts"],
      ["Tier 3: Investment-ready", "Business plan, proposal and investment readiness coaching; strategic partnerships; governance; deal brokerage", "Term loans with seasonal schedules; revenue-based finance; quasi-equity", "Finance secured and serviced"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**At Tier 0,** support focuses on group formation and governance, linkage to savings groups, financial literacy, and awareness of products and markets. Finance comes from savings groups, small grants and in-kind inputs. An enterprise graduates when it has regular sales and has kept group or individual records for six months." },
      { p: "**At Tier 1,** support covers bookkeeping and the use of mobile money, costing and pricing, quality and product development, and help with registration. Savings-linked micro-loans, matching grants and small asset leases provide finance. Graduation comes with registration, a first set of repeat orders from a buyer and basic records." },
      { p: "**At Tier 2,** support turns to supplier development with anchor buyers, quality assurance, standards and productivity, compliance with tax, VAT, insurance and certification requirements, contracting and negotiation, and cash-flow planning. Contract-backed working capital, receivables and asset finance and guaranteed loans become available. An enterprise graduates once it holds a contract or repeat orders for a full season, has tax clearance and keeps simple accounts." },
      { p: "**At Tier 3,** support consists of business planning, proposal and investment-readiness coaching, help with strategic partnerships, governance and deal brokerage. Finance comes from term loans with seasonal schedules, revenue-based finance and quasi-equity. The milestone is finance secured and serviced." },
    ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Gender-Transformative Approaches" },
  { b: [
    "**Group-to-enterprise pathways** that turn women's savings groups and producer groups into registered cooperatives at nodes B and C, so women capture aggregation and processing margins rather than staying at node A.",
    "**Collateral alternatives** that do not rely on land: contract and receivables security, movable-asset registration, group guarantees and savings-group credit histories.",
    "**Women's leadership** quotas and training in cooperatives, community enterprises and conservancy enterprise committees.",
    "**Care-aware delivery**: training scheduled and located to fit women's workloads, with childcare where needed, and mobile delivery in remote areas.",
    "**Gender-smart finance**: sex-disaggregated data from lenders and guarantee schemes; AFAWA and women's finance windows used as risk-sharing for women-led tourism MSMEs.",
    "**Cross-border trade support** for women small traders, including awareness of the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime between Zambia and Zimbabwe and advocacy for a SADC equivalent.",
    "**Household dialogue** on control of income and decision-making where enterprise growth may otherwise shift control away from women.",
    "**Diversification into higher-value chains**: targeted support for women in lodge-linked goods, guiding and nature-positive products, not only crafts.",
  ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Youth-Focused Incubation" },
  { b: [
    "**Destination-based incubators** in two or three priority hubs (for example Kasane, Livingstone-Victoria Falls and Katima Mulilo), run with BSOs and training institutions and, where possible, linked to existing initiatives such as SMEDCO's facilities or INAPEM's incubator network.",
    "**Skills-to-enterprise tracks** in guiding (certification and quality, responding to the guiding-quality bottleneck), transfers and logistics, waste and recycling services, and digital marketing and booking services for other MSMEs.",
    "**Apprenticeships with anchor operators** that lead to sub-contracts and the two or three successful bookings needed to be listed (MD p. 75).",
    "**Asset access** through lease-to-own schemes for vehicles, boats and equipment, linked to youth finance (YDF, EmpowerBank, bank youth windows).",
    "**Seed grants converting to loans** on milestones, so young entrepreneurs build a credit history.",
    "**Links to existing youth programmes**, such as the UNDP youth-led MSME programme in Livingstone and the youth priority in Namibia's SME fund, to avoid duplication.",
  ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Blended Support Packages" },
  { p: "Finance and technical assistance should be delivered together and tied to a buyer. Three packages are proposed:" },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Blended support packages",
    cols: [1800, 2300, 2700, 2226],
    head: ["Package", "Target", "Components", "Partners (indicative)"],
    rows: [
      ["Anchor supplier package", "Tier 2 suppliers in food, lodge-linked goods, transport and cleaning", "Supplier development TA; contract-backed working capital with payment ceded to the lender; partial guarantee; anchor payment-term commitment", "Anchor operators, banks, guarantee schemes, LEA and other BSOs"],
      ["Community enterprise package", "Cooperatives and community enterprises in nature-positive products and crafts", "Governance and certification support; grant for equipment; revolving fund; buyer brokerage", "Conservancies, CBOs, NGOs, MFIs, community funds"],
      ["Youth and women start-up package", "Tier 0-1 women- and youth-led enterprises", "Incubation; savings-group linkage; matching grant converting to loan; asset leasing; registration support", "BSOs, youth and women's finance institutions, training institutions"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**The anchor supplier package** targets Tier 2 suppliers in food, lodge-linked goods, transport and cleaning. It combines supplier-development technical assistance, contract-backed working capital in which payment is ceded to the lender, a partial guarantee and a commitment from the anchor on payment terms, delivered with anchor operators, banks, guarantee schemes, LEA and other BSOs." },
      { p: "**The community enterprise package** targets cooperatives and community enterprises in nature-positive products and crafts. It provides governance and certification support, a grant for equipment, a revolving fund and buyer brokerage, working with conservancies, CBOs, NGOs, MFIs and community funds." },
      { p: "**The youth and women start-up package** targets Tier 0 and Tier 1 enterprises led by women and young people. It offers incubation, savings-group linkage, a matching grant that converts to a loan, asset leasing and help with registration, delivered with BSOs, youth and women's finance institutions and training institutions." },
    ] },
  { h: 2, t: "BSO Strengthening" },
  { b: [
    "Develop and roll out a **common KAZA readiness diagnostic** and tier-tracking system for use by BSOs and financiers.",
    "Train BSO staff in **tourism supplier development**, R-NBT product development and **investment-readiness services** (loan packaging, lender introductions).",
    "Establish **BSO-financier referral agreements** so that BSO-assessed MSMEs receive faster processing.",
    "Extend outreach to KAZA hubs through **satellite offices or mobile advisers**.",
    "Build **gender and youth capacity** within BSOs, including staff training and disaggregated client data.",
    "Use **performance-based contracts** that pay BSOs for MSMEs graduating between tiers and accessing finance.",
    "**Strengthen BSOs' own systems** before asking them to deliver more: strategic plans, performance management and systems to assess and report the impact of services, which the PSDP survey found missing in Botswana's tourism association and apex body (Section 6.2).",
    "Adapt the **three-way supplier development model** (buyers, suppliers and mentoring consultants) used by UNDP's programme in Botswana, with lodges and DMCs as buyers and mentoring over six to ten months (Section 6.5).",
    "**Accredit mentors and advisers**, building on the PSDP proposals to coach CEDA's mentors and accredit LEA consultants, and use **retired-expert secondments** to add specialist skills at low cost.",
    "**Extend standards, testing and productivity services to the KAZA hubs** through alliances with BSOs that already have local offices, as the PSDP survey proposed for BOBS, and through company-level productivity programmes such as BNPC's *kaizen* programme.",
    "**Map BSO services in each country** and publish a directory and referral system, so that MSMEs and BSOs know who offers what and overlapping mandates can be reduced (Section 6.2).",
    "Strengthen **associations' advocacy capacity**, including sector or KAZA desks in chambers and apex bodies, so that MSME issues such as payment terms and the cost of compliance reach government and anchor buyers.",
  ] },
  { p: "The programme should work through existing BSOs rather than create new ones. A lead BSO and a first strengthening focus are proposed for each country, based on Section 7.3. Section 6.4 sets out, country by country, the tourism MSME needs that these BSOs should address, the recommended interventions and the partner BSOs for each." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Proposed lead BSOs and strengthening focus by country",
    cols: [1200, 2500, 2800, 2526],
    head: ["Country", "Lead and partner BSOs", "Strengthening focus", "Entry point"],
    rows: [
      ["Angola", "INAPEM, with the provincial governments of Cuando and Cubango and conservation NGOs", "Tourism and lodge-supply module in the incubator network; certification and registration help in Menongue; guiding and logistics capability", "INAPEM incubator network; Envolver+"],
      ["Botswana", "LEA, with BTO, HATAB, WIBA, Business Botswana, BOBS, BNPC and anchor operators", "Supplier development in Kasane and Maun; readiness diagnostic; LEA-CEDA referral and links to Stanbic finance; standards and productivity services through LEA; HATAB organisational strengthening", "Wilderness-LEA MoU; Wilderness-Stanbic facility"],
      ["Namibia", "NIPDB, with NACSO, IRDNC, NNF, NCCI regional branches and lodge operators", "KAZA window in the High Potential Pool and SME fund; conservancy enterprise to supplier pathway; registration support", "NIPDB High Potential Pool and SME fund"],
      ["Zambia", "ZDA and CEEC, with the Livingstone Tourism Association and training institutions", "Tourism supplier development in Livingstone; seasonal terms in CEEC products; youth incubation", "ZDA-CEEC agreement; UNDP youth MSME programme"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "SMEDCO, with ZIDA, ZTA, TBCZ and the CAMPFIRE Association", "Victoria Falls-Hwange supplier development; tax-clearance support through ZIDA; investment readiness for ZEEX", "SMEDCO programmes and ZSE partnership"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**In Angola,** INAPEM should lead, working with the provincial governments of Cuando and Cubango and conservation NGOs. The first focus would be a tourism and lodge-supply module in the incubator network, certification and registration help in Menongue, and building Angolan guiding and logistics capability, with the incubator network and Envolver+ as entry points. **In Botswana,** LEA should lead with BTO, HATAB, WIBA, Business Botswana, BOBS, BNPC and anchor operators, focusing on supplier development in Kasane and Maun, a readiness diagnostic, an LEA-CEDA referral pipeline and links to Stanbic finance, standards and productivity services delivered through LEA, and organisational strengthening for HATAB; the Wilderness-LEA memorandum and the Wilderness-Stanbic facility are the entry points." },
      { p: "**In Namibia,** NIPDB should lead with NACSO, IRDNC, the Namibia Nature Foundation, the chamber's regional branches and lodge operators. The focus would be a KAZA window in the High Potential Pool and the SME fund, a pathway from conservancy enterprise to supplier, and registration support. **In Zambia,** ZDA and CEEC should lead with the Livingstone Tourism Association and training institutions, focusing on tourism supplier development in Livingstone, seasonal terms in CEEC products and youth incubation, building on the ZDA-CEEC agreement and the UNDP youth MSME programme. **In Zimbabwe,** SMEDCO should lead with ZIDA, the Zimbabwe Tourism Authority, the Tourism Business Council and the CAMPFIRE Association, focusing on supplier development in Victoria Falls and Hwange, tax-clearance support through ZIDA and investment readiness for ZEEX, building on SMEDCO's programmes and its stock exchange partnership." },
    ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Financial Institution Strengthening" },
  { b: [
    "Technical assistance to partner banks and MFIs to design **seasonal products** and contract-backed supplier finance.",
    "**Sector training** for credit officers on R-NBT business models, anchor contracts and concession arrangements.",
    "**Standard tripartite contract templates** (MSME supplier, anchor buyer and lender) in which payment is ceded to the lender, following the Wilderness-Stanbic example, to reduce reliance on slow court enforcement.",
    "Support to guarantee schemes to open **tourism or KAZA windows** with seasonal terms, and to banks to use them.",
    "A **revenue-based finance pilot** with an impact fund or fintech, using anchor-buyer and digital payment data.",
    "**Gender-disaggregated reporting** and gender-smart product design.",
  ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Enabling Environment and Market Linkage Actions" },
  { p: "Most enabling-environment constraints are national and beyond the programme's control, but several can be influenced directly:" },
  { b: [
    "**Formalisation support at the point of need.** Fund BSO advisers to take MSMEs through registration and tax clearance when a contract requires it, using CIPA's online system in Botswana, ZIDA's one-stop centre in Zimbabwe and the BUE and Guiché Único in Angola.",
    "**Dialogue on MSME-friendly rules.** Use national validation workshops to raise specific issues with regulators: a simplified tax-clearance route for micro-suppliers in Zimbabwe, omnibus licensing for tourism in Zambia (already under discussion between the BRRA, the Ministry of Tourism and the Zambia Tourism Agency), faster registration in Namibia, and seasonal terms in public finance schemes.",
    "**Anchor procurement commitments.** Agree local and MSME procurement targets, product specifications and payment terms with anchor operators in each pilot destination, following the Wilderness and Gondwana examples, and report on them through the destination baselines (MD p. 140-141).",
    "**Cross-border supply.** Support the KAZA Secretariat in implementing the 2024 commitment to broaden the Univisa, and pilot a simplified procedure for small consignments from MSME suppliers across the Kazungula and Victoria Falls borders.",
    "**Update the regulatory evidence.** Replace the Doing Business 2020 benchmarks with B-READY data as the World Bank extends coverage to 164 economies by the end of 2026, and add KAZA-hub measures of registration and licensing time to the destination baselines.",
  ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Pathways to Financing" },
  { p: "The pathways connect the readiness tiers to the pilot mechanisms identified in the Financial Landscape Analysis:" },
  { b: [
    "**Tier 0-1 to savings-linked micro-finance:** savings groups linked to MFIs and women's and youth finance institutions, with community revolving funds where conservancy or CAMPFIRE revenues allow.",
    "**Tier 1-2 to grant-to-loan finance:** readiness grants that convert to loans or guarantees on milestones.",
    "**Tier 2 to contract-backed supplier finance and asset leasing:** anchored by lodge operators and DMCs, with tourism windows in national guarantee schemes.",
    "**Tier 3 to term finance, revenue-based finance and quasi-equity:** through banks, DFIs and impact investors, with seasonal repayment.",
  ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Priorities and Next Steps" },
  { b: [
    "**Start where demand is proven:** food and agricultural supply and lodge-linked goods in Kasane/Chobe, Hwange-Victoria Falls, Livingstone and the Zambezi Region, where anchor demand is confirmed.",
    "**Fix crafts through markets, not volume:** focus on product differentiation and buyer relationships before finance.",
    "**Build on what exists:** work through the lead BSOs and anchor supplier programmes in each country rather than creating parallel structures.",
    "**Close the gender and age data gap:** run a disaggregated MSME baseline in pilot destinations (Annex B).",
    "**Validate:** test readiness ratings, models and enabling-environment findings with MSMEs, BSOs, anchor buyers, regulators and financial institutions at national workshops.",
    "**Design pilots:** agree one or two blended packages per pilot destination with lead partners and term sheets for finance products.",
  ] },

  { h: 1, t: "Annex A: Evidence Map", numbered: false },
  { p: "The Macro Diagnostic sections used in this assessment are listed below by topic." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Macro Diagnostic sections used in this assessment",
    cols: [4626, 2000, 2400],
    head: ["Topic", "MD section", "MD pages"],
    rows: [
      ["Survey respondents by business activity", "10", "61"],
      ["Stakeholder findings by country (buyer and enterprise evidence)", "11", "65-76"],
      ["Emerging themes and triangulation", "12", "77-85"],
      ["Comparative value-chain table by country", "13", "87-94"],
      ["MSME definition", "14", "95-102"],
      ["Destination prioritisation", "15", "103-110"],
      ["Regenerative practice and enterprise cases", "17", "115-116"],
      ["Cross-border corridors", "18", "117-119"],
      ["Local value capture, procurement and market readiness", "20.7", "128-129"],
      ["Integrated preliminary conclusions", "21", "131-135"],
      ["Data gaps and baseline indicators", "22-23", "139-142"],
      ["Recommendations on value capture and supplier development", "24.1.5, 24.1.7", "146-148"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "The survey of respondents by business activity comes from Section 10 of the Macro Diagnostic (page 61), and the stakeholder findings by country, which provide the buyer and enterprise evidence, from Section 11 (pages 65 to 76). Emerging themes and triangulation are in Section 12 (pages 77 to 85), the comparative value-chain table by country in Section 13 (pages 87 to 94), the MSME definition in Section 14 (pages 95 to 102) and destination prioritisation in Section 15 (pages 103 to 110)." },
      { p: "Regenerative practice and the enterprise cases are in Section 17 (pages 115 and 116), and cross-border corridors in Section 18 (pages 117 to 119). Local value capture, procurement and market readiness are covered in Section 20.7 (pages 128 and 129), the integrated preliminary conclusions in Section 21 (pages 131 to 135), data gaps and baseline indicators in Sections 22 and 23 (pages 139 to 142), and the recommendations on value capture and supplier development in Sections 24.1.5 and 24.1.7 (pages 146 to 148)." },
    ] },

  { h: 1, t: "Annex B: Closing the Gender and Age Data Gap", numbered: false },
  { p: "A disaggregated MSME baseline in pilot destinations should collect the following for each enterprise, with a sample large enough to compare women- and men-led and youth- and adult-led enterprises within each priority value chain:" },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Proposed indicators for a disaggregated MSME baseline",
    cols: [2300, 6726],
    head: ["Area", "Indicators"],
    rows: [
      ["Owner and leadership", "Sex and age of owner(s) and manager; women's and youth share of ownership and of cooperative or committee leadership"],
      ["Value chain position", "Value chain; node (A-E); main buyers; share of sales to anchor buyers"],
      ["Business model", "Products; revenue by month; employees by sex and age; years operating"],
      ["Financial practices", "Separate business account; savings-group membership; sources of start-up and working capital; loans applied for and received; reasons for not applying"],
      ["Record-keeping", "Records kept (none, notebook, spreadsheet, accounting software); mobile-money and bank use for business"],
      ["Formality", "Registration; tax clearance; VAT; licences; insurance; certification; time and cost taken to register and obtain tax clearance"],
      ["Market access", "Number of anchor buyers; contracts held; payment terms; cross-border sales"],
      ["Constraints", "Self-reported constraints, including land and collateral, time and care responsibilities, mobility and safety"],
      ["Readiness tier", "Tier assigned using the common readiness diagnostic"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Owner and leadership:** the sex and age of the owner or owners and of the manager, and the share of ownership and of cooperative or committee leadership held by women and young people. **Value chain position:** the value chain, the node (A to E), the main buyers and the share of sales going to anchor buyers. **Business model:** products, revenue by month, employees by sex and age, and years in operation." },
      { p: "**Financial practices:** whether the enterprise has a separate business account, savings-group membership, the sources of start-up and working capital, loans applied for and received, and the reasons for not applying. **Record-keeping:** the records kept (none, a notebook, a spreadsheet or accounting software) and the use of mobile money and bank accounts for the business. **Formality:** registration, tax clearance, VAT, licences, insurance and certification, together with the time and cost taken to register and obtain tax clearance." },
      { p: "**Market access:** the number of anchor buyers, contracts held, payment terms and any cross-border sales. **Constraints:** self-reported constraints, including land and collateral, time and care responsibilities, mobility and safety. **Readiness tier:** the tier assigned using the common readiness diagnostic." },
    ] },
];

